WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.766
Detection logic should be understandable and maintainable.

00:00:02.766 --> 00:00:05.813
Clear naming conventions, readable conditions, consistent severity labels,

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:11.907
and descriptive metadata help analysts and future maintainers understand what the rule is meant to do.

00:00:11.907 --> 00:00:16.097
Documentation should explain the behavior being detected, the data sources required,

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:20.286
key assumptions, expected false positives, known limitations, and recommended triage steps.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:24.857
It should also point to relevant references or internal tickets when useful.

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:27.903
Readable logic matters because detections change over time.

00:00:27.903 --> 00:00:32.474
A rule may need tuning after a false positive, a telemetry change,

00:00:32.474 --> 00:00:37.425
a new business process, a platform migration, or a lesson from an incident.

00:00:37.425 --> 00:00:41.615
If nobody can understand the rule, nobody can improve it safely.

00:00:41.615 --> 00:00:43.900
Good rule writing also supports collaboration.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.185
Analysts, threat hunters, incident responders, engineers,

00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:50.756
and managers may all need to understand why a detection exists and

00:00:50.756 --> 00:00:52.660
how to interpret its output.
